On 21/10/2007, via RT golbeck @ cs. umd. edu  wrote:
> when you have a value for a hash, and then try to treat it as an array, it remembers
> the value. If we set it equal to something after pushing, the array is lost. I think
> the first behavior must be a bug.
>
>
> #!/usr/bin/perl
>
> #jen's perl bug.
> #xtqzkyo@cs.umd.edu
>
>
> %h = ();
> $h{1} = 3;
> push (@{$h{1}}, 2);
> push (@{$h{1}}, 4);

You're pushing values to the array @3 here.
Using "use strict" would prevent you to use named references like this.

> print $h{1};
> print "\\n";
> print @{$h{1}};
> print "\\n";
>
> $h{1} = 5;
>
> print $h{1};
> print "\\n";
> print @{$h{1}};

And here you're using array @5, which is a different array. Hence the
result you get is expected.

Note that hashes can't store arrays, only scalars. You probably want to
store arrays references in your hash instead.

